Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:14.520 align:start position:0%
 
you  hi I'm Simon Hays and I'm a production  sound mixer a production sound mixer in  film or television is the man or woman  that records the spoken dialogue and the  sound effects that actually happened  during the shoot of the film so it's my  responsibility to make sure that the  actors voices are recorded as cleanly as  possible with as little background noise  as possible and any sound effects that  are incidental while we're shooting are  also recorded so that afterwards the  sound design team can add my clean  dialogue recordings and my sound effects  recordings to more produce sound effects  recordings and music to create the final  soundtrack I work with a team and my  team are really important to me  filmmaking is always collaborative in  every department and I rely on my team  enormous ly getting production good  production sound isn't just down to the  production sound mixer you've got to  have a good boom operator a good second  boom operator the second boom operator  is the sound maintenance engineer so  generally the sound team on a normal  traditional movie will be four people it  will be the production sound mixer the  boom operator the sound maintenance  engineer who also swings the second boom  and the sound trainee who's obviously  training with us getting carpet down  underneath actors feet whenever we can  so that we don't have footsteps over the  dialogue and and generally learning the  ropes with us the boom operator has an  amazingly talented job to do he needs to  learn the dialogue as if he's an actor  because the boom needs to swing between  the dialogue if you've got different  different actors on set talking to each  other the boom needs to be moving  between them it can't just stay in the  middle because of course we're using a  directional mic to try and cancel out  the background noise that we don't want  to pick up so if the boom isn't pointing  exactly that the actors mouth the actor  is going to sound wide I'm going to have  to bring the gain up and the background  noise is going to come up so what the  boom operator has to do is cue the  microphone in between the dialogue but  not only that he needs to make sure that  the mic doesn't dip into frame because  of course if the mic dips into frame  then we have to do a retake if that was  a fantastic performance for the actor  then we've let the actor down and and  the director and everyone on the movie  set so the boom operator needs to know  his lens sizes he needs to know exactly  how close he can get to the to the edge  of frame because the closer he is to the  edge of frame the less gain or volume I  have to use and if I use less gain  volume then we get less background noise  if we get less background noise and  cleaner dialogue it means that a the the  performance sounds better in the cinema  but also it means that the sound design  team in the dub afterwards have an  opportunity to bring up the sound  effects and bring up the music more  because they've got better cleaner  dialogue to work with so everyone  benefits from having clean dialogue so  the boom operator if I can just give an  example if you've got an actor sitting  here in an actor sitting here the boom  operator will have the boom there and on  the lines he'll be cueing from one act  of speaking swinging across in between  the lines to the other act of speaking  if there's a third actor to that third  actor and then back to that actor on the  dialogue the sound maintenance engineer  again is the second boom operator when  we need to run two booms we get two  booms out we put them on two separate  tracks to give the sound editorial team  a chance to to marry those tracks  together it gives you twice as much  chance of getting great sound if you use  two booms the sound maintenance engineer  will also be responsible for making sure  day-to-day issues like the radio mic  channels when we use radio mics like the  one that I'm using here will make sure  that the radio mic channels are free for  the location we're working in making  sure that the actors are rigged with  radio mics when we need them before they  come onto set and generally being my  right-hand man so my team is really  important to me it's so collaborative  ladies ER habla was an incredibly  challenging process because it was  unique in many ways  Tom's vision of Lamy Zurab ler is a  completely Sun through musical so not  only is it a live sound recording but  also there's no dialogue when you think  about the traditional musical format for  filmmaking you have a song and dance  routine which the actors mind too then  you have a group of dialogue scenes that  tell a story and then you go back into  another song and dance routine so the  song and dance routines are broken up  with live dialogue scenes where you have  spoken dialogue storytelling Lamy zaraba  of course is sung from start to finish  so tom rightly made a decision that he  couldn't have the actors mining for two  and a half hours i just don't think  there would have been a connection with  the audience so what was incredible  about his vision was that he wanted to  give the actors the opportunity to sing  live but that's not the whole story what  was really exciting was he didn't want  them locked to tempo of course even if  you have actors sing live if you're  playing them back into a hidden earpiece  a pre-recorded piece of music it doesn't  matter that they're singing live they  still have to stick to tempo if they  want to pause for a moment and have a  have a think about an emotive issue in  their performance if they want to have a  moment to cry because of scenes sad or  even if they want to take a moment to  walk across a room before they sing  their next line if they're tied to a  pre-recorded tempo from a pre-recorded  piece of music they can't do that where  Tom's vision was extremely exciting and  unique was that not only did he want  them singing live but he wanted them to  be able to set the tempo so there was no  pre-recorded music at all what we did  was we had a pianist and the pianist  played into the ear pieces but the  pianist was asked by Tom to never ever  lead the actors the pianist was in a  soundproof box off the edge of the set  and she basically had him had a monitor  for each camera we were shooting three  cameras and she would watch the  performance and she would take her  you from the actors so that basically  what would happen was that acting took  precedent over the tempo of the music it  meant that on what have I done or  dreamed a dream  it meant that human Annie had enormous  scope to be emotive wherever they wanted  to whenever they thought their character  should take a pause or have a thought  they could do whatever they wanted to  what was so challenging and exciting  about that was the fact that afterwards  the orchestra was shown the final cut of  the movie and they played to that cut  so an orchestra on a normal film would  play to sheet music on lame is they  played to the actors performances on a  screen in front of them and I'm sure  that that has a huge huge effect on just  how emotive and the connection that the  audiences felt with those performances  that in turn gave the cast a huge  confidence that when they put in those  hugely emotional performances that they  would be recorded well enough to be used  in the final film  I'm proud of the whole of Lainey's there  was one scene in the movie that we had  to do to lip-sync and that was the scene  at the very beginning when the convicts  were pulling in the boat that scene was  unachievable to record life so what we  did we wild tracked the actors before  they shot the scene on the same  microphones that we shot the rest of the  movie on and we had to playback this  there they're singing through huge  speakers because we had wind machines  with VW beetle engines blowing their  hair everywhere we had wave machines  smashing them with waves as they were  putting that ship in but from that  moment when the ship arrives when  Russell Crowe first meets Hugh Jackman  from that moment for the rest of the  movie every solo vocal performance is  the performance that we recorded on the  set there was no rerecords those are the  real performances and I'm incredibly  proud of that but if there was one scene  that I'd like to talk about that really  does depict the challenges in  collaboration between the film crew the  actors and the sound and music team it  was on my own which Samantha barks sings  in the scene she's she's walking down  rue de plume a in Paris it's raining and  she basically does a hugely emotional  performance it's not allowed  performances it starts off very very  low-key under her breath  I walk with him till morning let's talk  about the first challenge which was the  rain we shot that on a soundstage which  which meant that we had to use false  rain it was a it was rain rig set up by  the special effects team the  collaboration with the special effects  team started with the fact that we told  them that we had to have a very very  fine spray of rain because of course the  bigger the rain droplets the more likely  it is that you're going to get a big  drop of rain hitting a microphone and  basically water logging it and ruining  the take the second problem with big  droplets of rain is the bigger the drop  of rain the more noise it's going to  make when it hits the cobblestones that  she's walk walking on and but and let's  let's bear in mind we can see the whole  street you're gonna hear the droplets of  rain hitting the rooftop of all of the  buildings around her every single hard  surface ok can potentially sound like a  drum when you have big drops of rain  hitting it so the first thing that we  did was we adjusted the spray of the  rain into an extremely fine spray we did  this with collaboration not just from  the special effects department but from  Tom Hooper and Danny Cohen his fantastic  DP Danny was looking through the cameras  we were adjusting the rain before we  even started rehearsing with Sam and  what we did was we brought the spray  down so that it was finer and finer and  finer until Danny said ok guys you've  gone too far now we can't actually see  that rain through camera and at that  point we brought the rain up just very  slightly so that Danny was happy that  the camera could read it so we knew then  that we had the whole set covered with  rain machines but we knew that the rain  was only just reading on camera and so  we diminished the sound of the droplets  hitting the microphone and all of the  hard surfaces as much as we possibly  could the next thing we did was we spoke  to Samantha and we told Samantha that we  wanted to use two radio mics at all  times so that we knew that if a droplet  of rain unluckily got straight into the  radio mic because you  you can see here here's my radio mic I'm  not going to touch it because it will  sound horrible  but if a droplet of rain suddenly lands  in that radio mic we wanted to have  another one to make sure that we could  protect that performance and not have to  cut the camera because of course it  wouldn't be fair on the actress not to  be able to give her a full take and to  keep cutting because of technical  difficulties so we rigged her with two  radio mics the next thing we did was we  spoke about my sound trainee his job on  that was we had a tent just out the back  of the set and he had a hairdryer in  that tent and what his job was to do was  every time Samantha finish to take he  would take those two radio mics off he'd  rerigged with another two radio mics  okay and while we were shooting the next  take he'd be drying the previous two  that he took off for the hairdryer okay  so that we knew that we had basically  dry new radio mics on every single take  that only tells part of the story we  then started talking about the boom we  obviously we like to give the sound  design team a choice afterwards on the  whole of lamia's and that choice was do  you want to use the LaValley a which is  the radio mic or do you want to use a  boom that choice is a choice that's best  made in the dub when the film has been  cut when we could understand the  orchestration and how loud it needed to  go it's a it's certainly a choice that's  best made afterwards rather than on the  set what a good production sound mixer  does is he gives a great radio might  track in a great boom track so that  choice can be made so the boom we found  was over Sam's head and it's running  around about it's running there just on  top of her forehead just out of frame  and what the boom operator did was we  had the second boom operator and in he  was booming over the top of the first  boom operator but he didn't have a  microphone on the end of his pole what  he actually had was a roof okay  he had a 1 foot square okay rooftop  which he hovered via his boom over the  top of the main boom and so you've got  the microphone and then you've got the  second boom shielding that microphone  from the droplets of rain so that the  rains landing on the top of the roof so  it's not landing on the microphone  so what we got out of that scene was  just like the rest of lame is we got  hugely emotive performance that Samantha  didn't have to re-record  that scene the way that you hear it in  the movie was the scene that Samantha  sang on the movie set  and it was hugely inspiring to be able  to capture that under those  circumstances

